<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ilghazi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1122">1122</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Najm al-Din Ilghazi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ilghazi was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> emir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqid dynasty</a> who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a> and took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1117–1118. In 1119 he destroyed the army of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ager-sanguinis">Battle of Ager Sanguinis</a>. In 1121 he and his allies were defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> IV at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-didgori">Battle of Didgori</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Najm al-Din Ilghazi ibn Artuq was born into the Döğer tribe of the Oghuz. His father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuq-ibn-ekseb">Artuk Bey</a>, founder of the dynasty, had been made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> by the Seljuq emir Tutush, and Ilghazi and his brother Sökmen succeeded him there. In the wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duqaq">Duqaq</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and Radwan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> he sided with Duqaq, was imprisoned after a quarrel, and recovered Jerusalem on his release, holding it until the Fatimid vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal-shahanshah">al-Afdal Shahanshah</a> took it in 1098. He then served the Seljuq sultan as shihna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> until 1104.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Sökmen's death in 1104 he led the Artukid family and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a> from Sökmen's son Ibrahim in 1108. He made no lasting alliances, siding with Muslims or crusaders as it suited him. In 1114 he defeated Aqsunqur al-Bursuqi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, and in 1115 he rebelled against the sultan with his father-in-law Toghtekin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and joined Roger, whom he later killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ager-sanguinis">Ager Sanguinis</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin I of Jerusalem</a> against the Seljuq general Bursuq ibn Bursuq. Invited by Amina Khatun after the murder of Lu'lu' in 1117, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and in 1118 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ager-sanguinis">Ager Sanguinis</a> the Antiochene towns of Atharib, Zerdana, Sarmin, Ma'arrat al-Numan and Kafartab fell to him, but Baldwin II drove him back at the Battle of Hab on 14 August 1119. The next year he took Nisibin and ravaged the County of Edessa, enslaving much of its population; Matthew of Edessa describes villages burned and clergy killed. After Didgori, where Matthew of Edessa puts the dead at 400,000, only he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubays-ibn-sadaqa">Dubays ibn Sadaqa</a> escaped among the leaders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1122 he and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belek-ghazi">Balak</a> captured Joscelin I of Edessa, and he died that November at Diyarbekir and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a>. Balak succeeded him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and his sons Suleiman and Timurtash in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a>. Ibn al-Qalanisi, mostly neutral, faults only his habit of staying drunk for days, and one account holds that his drunkenness kept him from exploiting his victory of 1119. Walter the Chancellor, captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ager-sanguinis">Ager Sanguinis</a>, calls him a tyrant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ilghazi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 12 August 1121, at the Battle of Didgori, his army routed a large Muslim coalition led by the Artuqid ruler <b>Ilghazi</b>, and in 1122 he captured Tbilisi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At Didgori, west of Tbilisi, King David IV the Builder of Georgia defeated a large Seljuk-led Muslim army under <b>Ilghazi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-12">12 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its most famous holder was Belek Ghazi, a nephew of <b>Ilghazi</b>, who captured Joscelin I of the County of Edessa in 1122 and King Baldwin II of Jerusalem in 1123 and imprisoned them there.</blockquote><a href="/state/artuqids-of-harput">Artuqids of Harput</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ilghazi</b>, son of the Turkmen commander Artuq, took Mardin in the early 12th century and made it the base of his branch of the family.</blockquote><a href="/state/artuqids-of-mardin">Artuqids of Mardin</a></li><li><blockquote>Its best-documented ruler is Toghan Arslan, nicknamed the Hunchback, who held Bitlis and Erzen in the early 12th century, allied with the Artuqid <b>Ilghazi</b> against the Georgians and the Crusaders, and fought his neighbours over Mayyafariqin.</blockquote><a href="/state/dilmacogullari">Dilmaçoğulları</a></li><li><blockquote>Threatened by the Franks, Aleppo's notables turned to outside protectors, and in 1117-1118 the city passed to the Artuqid <b>Ilghazi</b>, later part of the Artuqids of Mardin's sphere and then the Zengid dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/seljuk-emirate-of-aleppo">Seljuk Emirate of Aleppo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakır</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.92,40.22&amp;year=1112" aria-label="Diyarbakır on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5553 801 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ilghazi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6003 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6003 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.92,40.22&amp;year=1112">What was here in 1112? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
